## Key Concepts for Node.js Password Authentication

This document outlines the core steps and security principles for building a user login system with Node.js, Express, and bcrypt.

### Project Setup

- **Initialize Project**: `npm init -y`
- **Dependencies**:
  - `express`: The web server framework.
  - `bcrypt`: For hashing and comparing passwords.
- **Dev Dependencies**:
  - `nodemon`: Automatically restarts the server on file changes.

### User Registration and Security

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Never store passwords in plain text.** A database breach would expose every user's password.

Passwords must be cryptographically hashed and salted.

- **Hashing**: A one-way function that transforms a password into a fixed-length string of characters (a hash). It is computationally difficult to reverse.
- **Salting**: Adding a unique, random string (the salt) to each password before hashing it. This ensures that even if two users have the same password, their stored hashes will be different, preventing rainbow table attacks.

### Implementing Secure Hashing with `bcrypt`

`bcrypt` handles both salting and hashing in one asynchronous step. Use a `try...catch` block to handle potential errors.

```javascript
// Inside an async POST /users route
try {
  // bcrypt handles salt generation automatically with the 'rounds' parameter
  const hashedPassword = await bcrypt.hash(req.body.password, 10);
  const user = { name: req.body.name, password: hashedPassword };
  users.push(user);
  res.status(201).send();
} catch {
  res.status(500).send();
}
```

> [!NOTE]
> The second argument to `bcrypt.hash()` is the number of **cost factor** (or rounds). The default is `10`. A higher number is more secure but takes more time to compute.

### User Login and Password Verification

To verify a user's login attempt, you must compare the plain-text password they provide with the stored hash.

> [!TIP]
> Always use `bcrypt.compare()` for verification. It is specifically designed to be slow and run in constant time to **prevent timing attacks**, where an attacker could analyze response times to guess parts of a password.

```javascript
// Inside an async POST /users/login route
const user = users.find(user => user.name === req.body.name);
if (user == null) {
  return res.status(400).send('Cannot find user');
}

try {
  if (await bcrypt.compare(req.body.password, user.password)) {
    res.send('Success');
  } else {
    res.send('Not Allowed');
  }
} catch {
  res.status(500).send();
}
```

This process ensures that the plain-text password is never exposed or stored on the server during the comparison.
